Author: Dilip Dalton <dilipdalton@hotmail.com>
Date:   Fri Sep 11 09:51:42 2026 -0700

    store+lean: the publish read every file TWICE, and the fix that was "impossible" wasn't
    
    lean's compose hashed the whole file, then handed the same file to the
    store to read again part by part. On the 2026-09-10 door drill that
    pre-pass was roughly 11 s of the `mixed` workload's 24.3 s — all of it
    at the disk's read ceiling, none of it on the wire.
    
    `ComposeSpec::crc64: None` already asked the store to accumulate while
    uploading, and forge used it. lean could not, because of a constraint I
    wrote into this crate earlier today: accumulating pins the upload to a
    sequential loop, "a CRC-64 taken out of order is a different number", so
    `par = if acc.is_some() { 1 }`. True of a streaming accumulator. Not
    true of CRC-64.
    
    `crc64_combine(a, b, len_b)` gives the CRC of `A||B` from the two CRCs
    and B's length, no bytes re-read. For a reflected CRC whose init and
    xorout are both all-ones the init terms cancel — `Z(X) ^ Z(I) = Z(X ^ I)
    = Z(0) = 0` — leaving `F(A||B) = Z_n(F(A)) ^ F(B)`; structure follows
    zlib's `crc32_combine` widened to 64 bits. So parts are hashed
    INDEPENDENTLY and folded in part order, and both compose arms now share
    one checksum path instead of two that could disagree.
    
    The `par` gate is gone. lean gets the single read AND the parallel
    upload; forge gets the parallel upload it was excluded from by
    construction. Neither had to choose.
    
    The sort before the fold is now load-bearing twice over — S3 wants
    ascending part numbers, and `crc64_combine` composes A-then-B, so a grid
    folded in completion order is the checksum of a different object.
    `folding_out_of_order_yields_a_different_object` asserts the shuffle
    does NOT coincidentally agree, because a sort nothing can move is a sort
    nobody will keep.
    
    Without the pre-pass lean has no checksum of its own, so the 412
    AdoptOwn recognizer reads the file on that rare path instead, and a
    compose that returns no checksum now REFUSES: `a_compose_that_reports_
    no_checksum_refuses_instead_of_citing` drives a backend that strips the
    header and asserts nothing is cited. Inventing a number there would cite
    bytes nothing vouched for.
    
    The many-part test first failed on its own arithmetic — the grid summed
    to 9001 of 10000 — caught by the "the grid must cover the object"
    assertion put there for exactly that. Four mutations, each failing its
    test. flint-store 25 passed, flint-lean 173 passed.
    
